<header><p class="enc-kicker">Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Stato da Mar</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Stato da Màr</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Stato da Mar ('State of the Sea') was the collective name for the overseas possessions of the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, as distinct from its Italian mainland territories, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/domini-di-terraferma">Domini di Terraferma</a>. Built up from the 11th century and especially after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-crusade">Fourth Crusade</a> of 1204, it included Istria, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a>, coastal Albania, the Ionian Islands, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> and ports and islands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a>, protecting Venice's trade with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/stato-da-mar.jpg" alt="Stato da Mar" width="320" height="227" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Maximilian Dörrbecker (Chumwa) · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Venezianische_Kolonien.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
